% A first wordgenx document: wordgenx build examples/hello.wgx
\document[lang=en-GB]
\meta[title="Hello, wordgenx", author="wordgenx contributors", keywords=(docx, markup)]
\section[size=A4, margins=2.5cm]
\use{styles/house.wgxs}

\let{product}{wordgenx}
\define{note}[title="Note"]{\b{#title:} #body}

\p[style=Title]{Hello, \product}

= Introduction

\product compiles plain text to "idiomatic" Word documents --- with real
styles, real list numbering, and *strong*, _emphasised_ or `code` text.
See [the project page](https://example.com/wordgenx) or <https://example.org>.

\note[title="Tip"]{Macros expand to ordinary markup.}

== Lists

- Bullets use Word's own list numbering
- Nested items work too
  - like this one
1. Numbered lists restart
2. for every new list
   a. with lettered sub-items

\list[Legal]{
1. Clauses use the _Legal_ numbering from the style sheet.
   1. Sub-clauses are lettered.
2. Second clause.

   A second paragraph in the same clause.
}

=* Unnumbered heading

\align[center]{Centred text with \font[color=theme.accent2, size=14pt]{direct formatting},
\u[double]{underlines}, H\sub{2}O and E = mc\sup{2}.}

